This is the foundation set.
In Lesson Set 01 you will build and test the individual sensors one at a time, turn their raw output into clean ROS2 topics, fuse the related sensors, and create simple state nodes that the rest of the system can listen to.
By the end of this set you will have:
-Working sketches and ROS2 nodes for each sensor
-A motion fusion node that combines PIR and RCWL
-A distance fusion node that combines Ultrasonic and Time-of-Flight
-Separate state nodes for motion and distance

These state nodes are what the OLED (Lesson Set 02) and future modules will subscribe to. That is why we keep them simple and stable.
What is inside this set

PIR Sensor Module
-mcu/sensor module + Sketch + ROS2 node. Digital motion detection.

RCWL Sensor Module
-mcu/sensor module + Sketch + ROS2 node. Microwave motion detection.

Motion Fusion Node
-Combines PIR and RCWL into a single, more reliable motion signal.

Ultrasonic Sensor Module
-mcu/sensor module + Sketch + ROS2 node. Classic distance measurement.

Time-of-Flight (ToF) Sensor Module
-mcu/sensor module + Sketch + ROS2 node. More precise distance measurement.

Distance Fusion Node
-Combines Ultrasonic and ToF into a cleaner distance value.

State Nodes
-Motion State Node
-Distance State Node
These take the fused data and publish simple, usable states that higher-level modules can act on.

How to work through this set
-Do the modules in order.
-Each one has its own plain text document with wiring, code, and expected results.
-Get each sensor working by itself before you move to fusion.
-Get the fusion nodes working before you move to the state nodes.

When you finish Lesson Set 01, you will have a solid, modular sensor layer.

Lesson Set 02 (OLED) will simply listen to the state nodes you create here.

Lesson Set 03 (sensor arrays) will replace the individual sensors without forcing you to rewrite the display or the state logic.
That is the design on purpose.

Start with the PIR module when you are ready.
